    "question": {
      "source_id": "unreconciled:step-q221691",
      "problem": "Consider the following false statement.\n\n$9n-4$ is even for any $n\\in Z$ \n\nA counterexample for this statement would be an integer {{field-1}} such that {{field-2}} is {{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in Z,\\,9n-4\\text{ is even}.\n$$\n\nSince this statement is false, its negation is true. \n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true. \n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in Z,\\,9n-4\\text{ is even})\\,≡\\,∃n\\in Z,\\,9n-4\\text{ is odd}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $n$ such that $9n-4$ is $\\text{odd}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
